    {
      "id": "grammar-definite-article-body-part-owner-clear",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "verbo + el/la/los/las + parte del cuerpo (dueño claro)",
      "questionEn": "Why does Spanish sometimes use the word for “the” before a body part where English uses “my,” “your,” or “their”?",
      "answerEn": "When the context already makes the owner clear, Spanish often uses el, la, los, or las before the body part instead of a possessive word such as mi or tu.",
      "noteEn": "Choose el, la, los, or las to match the body part, not its owner: la mano is feminine singular, while los brazos is masculine plural. The verb or another short word in context can identify whose body part it is. If the owner would not otherwise be clear, Spanish can still use a possessive word.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Lucía levanta la mano.",
        "en": "Lucía raises her hand."
      },
      "tags": [
        "grammar",
        "definite article",
        "body parts",
        "possession",
        "A2"
      ],
      "reviewStatus": "ai-authored-internal-qa",
